  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/phallichthys_tico_en/",
      "html": "<p>Males reach a maximum length of about 2.5 cm, females 4.5 cm.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/phallichthys_tico_en/",
      "html": "<p>This is a top rarity among the livebearers. No commercial fishing for ornamental fish exists in its range, so the species is imported only occasionally by travelling enthusiasts. Until now it has proved impossible to build up a stable aquarium strain from these few animals, so the species always vanished from the hobby after only a few generations. The fish offered here are German-bred. Keeping them is rather unproblematic.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/phallichthys_tico_en/",
      "html": "<p>The species is restricted to the upper reaches of the river San Juan in Costa Rica and Nicaragua.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/phallichthys_tico_en/",
      "html": "<p>The fish always need very clean water of low microbiological contamination. Water chemistry is not very important, but extremes should be avoided. Best is a pH of 7-7.5, a hardness of 3-10°GH and a temperature of 24-28°C.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/phallichthys_tico_en/",
      "html": "<p>The fish take frozen and dried food readily, but they also have to be fed some plant material.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/phallichthys_tico_en/",
      "html": "<p>Lexicon: Phallichthys: ancient Greek, means “penisfish”. tico: after the indigenous people of Costa Rica who call themselves “Tico”.</p>"
    }
  ],
